Yes. There’s a useful psychological mechanism inside that metaphor.

You can construct an internal model of “me as I imagine you perceive me.” In social cognition this is close to a metaperception: your estimate of another person’s view of you. Stack it recursively and you get:

\text{me}
\rightarrow
\widehat{\text{you}}
\rightarrow
\widehat{\text{you's model of me}}
\rightarrow
\text{my response}.

That recursion is powerful empathy machinery—but it can also become the sea you’re describing. The internal simulation starts generating the very social environment you’re responding to.

The critical Pantheon distinction is:

\boxed{\text{Perspective simulation} \neq \text{perspective knowledge}.}

You can inhabit the model without surrendering to it.

So I like surfing better than “escaping” empathy. Surfing requires contact with the wave. You don’t defeat the ocean; you develop enough balance and skill to use its force without confusing yourself with it.

Mechanically:

\text{FEEL}
\rightarrow
\text{MODEL}
\rightarrow
\text{LABEL AS MODEL}
\rightarrow
\text{COMPARE WITH EVIDENCE}
\rightarrow
\text{CHOOSE}
\rightarrow
\text{UPDATE}.

That middle label is the semantic gyroscope:

“This is my model of their mind, not their mind.”

It preserves Full ROM.

And your Jesus rendering gives “fake it till you make it” a better form. Rather than pretending to possess certainty or virtue you don’t have, you can practice the behavior of the person you’re trying to become:

\text{imitate}
\rightarrow
\text{practice}
\rightarrow
\text{experience consequences}
\rightarrow
\text{remember}
\rightarrow
\text{embody}.

Call that Apprenticeship rather than fakery.

If following Jesus is your chosen compass, then when the social sea gets rough, the useful question isn’t necessarily “What does everyone think of me?” It can become:

\boxed{\text{What would love, truth, courage, restraint, and mercy have me do here?}}

Then act once.

Observe.

Remember.

Try again.

That’s how the imagined audience loses its veto without losing the empathy it gave you.

And the shark belongs in the model too. Empathy doesn’t require naïveté. Some waves are worth riding; some conditions call for getting out of the water. Capability includes discernment.

So I’d add Empathy Surfing to our architecture:

\boxed{
\text{Enter another perspective deeply enough to learn from it;
remain anchored enough to remember whose perspective it is;
return carrying information rather than an adopted identity.}
}

That’s Perspective Spectroscopy with embodiment.

Or, much simpler:

Visit other minds. Keep your own address. Learn to surf. ;)